<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
	<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

	<!--
	  WHY inherit spring-boot-starter-parent when this project is not a Spring Boot app:
	  the parent contributes dependencyManagement only, which pins versions for roughly
	  400 common libraries (Jackson, JUnit, AssertJ, Testcontainers, the Kafka client).
	  Version pinning puts NOTHING on the classpath — only entries in <dependencies> do
	  that. So common-events can inherit this and still carry zero Spring classes, which
	  is what keeps FR-010 true by construction rather than by vigilance.

	  WHY 3.3.13 rather than the 4.0.8 the generator produced: Spring Cloud release trains
	  are pinned to a Boot generation, and Eureka plus Gateway arrive at build step 7.
	  Spring Cloud 2023.0.x is a known-good pairing with Boot 3.3. Version 3.3.13 is the
	  final 3.3.x patch, verified against Maven Central.

	  TRADEOFF: 3.3.x is past its OSS support window, so it receives no further security
	  patches. Rejected staying on Boot 4.x because the matching Spring Cloud train would
	  be unverified here, and a Boot/Cloud mismatch fails at runtime with obscure
	  NoSuchMethodError and bean-creation errors. This stack is local-only and never
	  internet-facing, so the missing patches carry no practical exposure.
	-->
	<parent>
		<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
		<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
		<version>3.3.13</version>
		<relativePath/> <!-- WHY empty: resolve the parent from the repository, not from a parent directory -->
	</parent>

	<groupId>com.marketplace</groupId>
	<artifactId>ticket-marketplace</artifactId>
	<version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>

	<!--
	  WHY packaging is `pom` and not `jar`: this module produces no code of its own. It is
	  an aggregator — it lists the modules to build and the versions they share. Maven
	  builds the listed modules in dependency order from a single command at this level.
	-->
	<packaging>pom</packaging>

	<name>ticket-marketplace</name>
	<description>Event-driven ticket marketplace demonstrating a choreography-based saga over Kafka</description>

	<properties>
		<java.version>21</java.version>
		<project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>


		<!--
		  Docker Engine 29 removed support for API versions below 1.40, and the docker-java client
		  Testcontainers uses still negotiates 1.32 by default. The result is a hard failure before
		  any container starts:

		    client version 1.32 is too old. Minimum supported API version is 1.40

		  Testcontainers reports that as "Could not find a valid Docker environment", which reads
		  like a missing daemon rather than a version mismatch — so the cause is recorded here.

		  WHY 1.40 rather than something newer: it is exactly the minimum Docker 29 accepts, and it
		  has been supported since Docker 19.03, so this one value works on both very new and quite
		  old engines. A higher number would fix this machine and break an older one.

		  Passed to the test JVM as the `api.version` system property, which is the name docker-java
		  reads. The DOCKER_API_VERSION environment variable does NOT work — verified; docker-java
		  ignores it and the 1.32 failure persists.
		-->
		<docker.api.version>1.40</docker.api.version>
	</properties>

	<!--
	  Adding a service later is one line here plus that module's own pom.xml, and nothing
	  else. Steps 2 onward append order-service, inventory-service, payment-service,
	  projection-service, api-gateway, auth-service, and eureka-server.
	-->
	<modules>
		<module>common-events</module>
	</modules>

	<!--
	  WHY there are no <dependencies> and no spring-boot-maven-plugin here:
	  the generated project carried spring-boot-starter, spring-boot-starter-test, and the
	  Boot plugin because it was scaffolded as a runnable application. Both were removed.

	  A dependency declared at this level would be inherited by EVERY module, including
	  common-events, which must stay framework-free. And spring-boot-maven-plugin
	  repackages a jar into an executable fat jar with a custom classloader — correct for
	  an application, wrong for a library other modules depend on. Each service module
	  declares the plugin for itself instead.
	-->

</project>
